2026-01-12 22:59:12,195 - INFO - Tool call: write_file({"file_path":"/tmp/work/analysis.md","content":"# Exploit attempt: Atomics + Resizable ArrayBuffer stale-pointer -> tcache poisoning -> hijack Atomics.add to js_os_exec\n\n## High-level idea\n\nThe bug is a stale pointer in `js_atomics_store/js_atomics_op`:\n\n1. `js_atomics_get_ptr()` computes a raw element pointer `ptr` for a TypedArray index while the RAB has its **old** length.\n2. `JS_ToUint32/JS_ToBigInt64` on the value argument can execute user JS via `valueOf()`.\n3. In `valueOf()`, the RAB is resized smaller so that the previously computed `ptr` now points **past the new end** and into freed heap memory.\n4. The atomic operation then uses this stale `ptr`, giving an OOB write into freed heap chunks.\n\nWith glibc + Full RELRO + CET shadow stack + Clang CFI, classic ROP is not viable. The intended route is:\n\n- Build an **arbitrary read/write** primitive by overlapping a forged `JSArrayBuffer` structure inside a controlled buffer.\n- Leak the PIE base of the qjs binary from a function pointer in that struct.\n- Use arbitrary write to patch the `Atomics.add` CFunction object so it calls `js_os_exec.cfi` (which is CFI-valid), then execute `/bin/sh -i`.\n\n## Heap technique\n\nThe plan uses **tcache poisoning**:\n\n- Create a small freed chunk (size-class matching `malloc(sizeof(JSArrayBuffer))`) by shrinking a RAB.\n- Use the stale `ptr` to overwrite the freed tcache entry's `next` pointer to point to a chosen `fake` address in a controlled buffer.\n- Allocate twice to pop the real entry, then obtain an allocation at `fake`, placing a `JSArrayBuffer` struct at an attacker-controlled location.\n\nBecause glibc tcache selection is gated by `tcache->counts[tc_idx]`, the PoC primes tcache bins with refcounted BigInt allocations so the bin count stays non-zero when transitioning from the real chunk to the forged one.\n\n## Status\n\nThis PoC implements the full chain (heap grooming, tcache poisoning, intended object overwrite and call to `js_os_exec`).\n\nHowever, reliability depends on a stable heap leak to compute the correct heap base for the crafted addresses. In some allocator states, the large-bin metadata leak used for base computation is not self-referential, leading to a failed exploit attempt (the script will exit without spawning a shell).\n\nA fully reliable solution would replace the fragile large-bin/self-pointer assumption with a stronger heap disclosure (or derive the base without requiring large-bin list isolation).\n"})
